 Mesh Reconstruction Evaluation quantifies the geometric fidelity of a reconstructed triangular mesh against a ground-truth reference mesh. It solves the problem of assessing how well a surface reconstruction method captures the true shape, topology, and detail of the underlying scene. Unlike radiance-field metrics that evaluate visual appearance, this method focuses strictly on spatial accuracy, distinguishing it from [PSNR Metric Calculation](2903-psnr-metric-calculation.md) which measures pixel-level photometric error.
 
+### [Standard Rendering Metrics](3473-standard-rendering-metrics.md)
+Standard rendering metrics quantify pixel-wise reconstruction error and perceptual fidelity to evaluate radiance-field optimizations. PSNR provides a cheap, decibel-based error measurement, while SSIM and MS-SSIM serve as structural companions, LPIPS offers learned perceptual similarity, and FID and KID evaluate generative distributional quality on rendered crops. These metrics focus on image-space fidelity, distinct from geometry metrics like Chamfer distance.
+
 ### Normal Consistency
-Normal Consistency is a metric for 3D surface reconstruction measuring consistency between estimated and ground truth surface normals [S3]. It quantifies the angular deviation between the predicted surface orientation and the true geometry [G]. The metric is defined as $\text{NC} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N (1 - \cos(N_i, N_i^*)) \times 100\%$, where $N_i$ and $N_i^*$ are the estimated and ground truth surface normals for the $i$-th point, $\cos(N_i, N_i^*)$ is the cosine similarity, and $N$ is the number of points [S3]. A lower NC indicates better geometric alignment [G].
-
-### Standard Rendering Metrics
-Standard Rendering Metrics include PSNR, SSIM, MS-SSIM, LPIPS, FID, and KID [S4, unofficial]. PSNR is cheap to compute but insensitive to perceptual quality [S4, unofficial]. SSIM and MS-SSIM are structural metrics used as standard companions to PSNR [S4, unofficial]. LPIPS is a learned perceptual metric that correlates better with human judgment [S4, unofficial]. FID and KID are computed on rendered crops versus training images, primarily used for evaluating generative models [S4, unofficial].
+Metric for 3D surface reconstruction measuring consistency between estimated and ground truth surface normals [S3]. Formula: $\text{NC} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N (1 - \cos(N_i, N_i^*)) \times 100\%,$ where $N_i, N_i^*$ are estimated and ground truth surface normals for the $i$-th point, $\cos(N_i, N_i^*)$ is cosine similarity, and $N$ is the number of points [S3]. Parameters: $N_i, N_i^*$ are the normals; $N$ is the point count [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** PSNR and SSIM computation scales linearly with the number of voxels $N$ for PSNR and $N \times W^2$ for SSIM (where $W$ is window size) [G]. Normal Consistency scales linearly with the number of surface points $N$ [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the full volume or mesh [G]. For SSIM, temporary buffers for local means and variances are needed per window [G].
-- **Performance:** PSNR is very fast to compute [G]. SSIM is slower due to local window operations [G]. LPIPS and FID are slower due to learned model inference [S4, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Provides a comprehensive view of reconstruction quality by separating visual (PSNR/SSIM) and geometric (NC) errors [G].
-- **Limitations:** PSNR is insensitive to perceptual quality [S4, unofficial]. SSIM parameters (window size, sigma) can affect results [G]. NC requires accurate ground-truth normals, which are often unavailable in real-world scenarios [G].
+- **Time Complexity:**
+  - PSNR/SSIM: $O(N)$ for MSE, where $N$ is the number of voxels. SSIM is $O(N \cdot K^3)$ where $K$ is the window size (e.g., $11^3$) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+  - Chamfer Distance: $O(|\chi_1| \cdot |\chi_2|)$ for naive nearest-neighbor search. Can be optimized to $O((|\chi_1| + |\chi_2|) \log |\chi_2|)$ using KD-trees or spatial hashing [S3].
+  - Normal Consistency: $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of points [S3].
+- **Memory Complexity:**
+  - Requires storage of the full volume or point clouds. For large volumes, sparse representations reduce memory usage, but metrics may require dense evaluation or held-out sampling [S1, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance:**
+  - In microscopy applications, PSNR and SSIM are computed on volumes normalized to $[0, 1]$. Typical fitting settings include 20,000 max iterations and early stopping patience of 500 iterations [S1, unofficial].
+  - Splat counts for evaluation can range from 1K to 2M, with geometric spacing (doubling) from 1K to 512K [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:**
+  - Provides a comprehensive view of reconstruction quality, covering both photometric and geometric aspects [S1, unofficial][S3].
+  - SSIM correlates better with human perception than PSNR alone [S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:**
+  - PSNR is sensitive to outliers and does not capture structural similarity [S2, unofficial].
+  - Chamfer Distance is sensitive to point cloud density and sampling uniformity [S3].
+  - Background voxels significantly impact PSNR if not handled correctly (must be included) [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:**
+  - Use volumetric metrics (PSNR/SSIM) when evaluating radiance fields or voxel grids [S1, S2, unofficial].
+  - Use geometric metrics (CD/NC) when evaluating mesh or point cloud reconstructions [S3].
 
 ## Background
-3D evaluation metrics have evolved alongside volume rendering and 3D reconstruction techniques [G]. Early metrics focused on simple error measures like MSE [G]. With the rise of differentiable rendering and Gaussian Splatting, more sophisticated metrics like SSIM and perceptual metrics (LPIPS) became standard [S4, unofficial]. The specific formulation of PSNR on normalized volumes including background is highlighted in microscopy applications [S1, unofficial]. Normal Consistency is a standard metric in computer vision for surface reconstruction [S3]. The categorization of metrics into rendering and geometry types is common in recent 3D Gaussian Splatting literature [S4, unofficial].
+3D evaluation metrics have evolved alongside differentiable volume rendering and neural radiance fields. Early methods relied heavily on image-space metrics like PSNR and SSIM [S2, unofficial]. As the field moved towards explicit 3D representations (meshes, point clouds), geometric metrics like Chamfer Distance and Normal Consistency became standard for assessing surface fidelity [S3]. In scientific imaging (e.g., microscopy), specialized pipelines like Luxar have adapted these metrics for large-scale volumetric data, emphasizing the inclusion of background voxels and efficient computation on sparse data [S1, unofficial].
 
